MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=3
RELEASE_VERSION=0
PRERELEASE=1
DEV_VERSION=13
RERELEASE=0
DESCRIPTION=KeyQuorum refusal reports stay coarse and the recipient's audit names the cause. The receive path in server file passes the checkpoint's own result to fl_kq_report_refusal_cp_in. The signed KQER report, which is carried to the sender, keeps the coarse class through fl_kq_cp_to_result (malformed as Framing, a missing key as Untrusted, too large as Denied/resource, every signature, trust, replay, freshness, fork, threshold or permit refusal as Denied/policy), so a report is not an oracle for the sender (KeyQuorum #169). The precise cause (cause=bad-signature, cause=replayed, cause=forked and the rest) is written only to the recipient's local audit line. Local problems (the destination exists, an I/O error, no driver) are still not reported. fl_kq_report_refusal_in and the checkpoint form share one writer. test_kq_service covers every cause, checks that a replay and a bad signature sign the same code and class, and checks the unreported results. Version policy: an optional RERELEASE flag (0 or 1, after DEV_VERSION) lets a row reuse an original row's (MAJOR, STANDARD, RELEASE, DEV_VERSION) on purpose, so a PR stacked on another PR takes its base's DEV_VERSION in its own .ver; check_version_entries_semver_dev_unique.sh requires exactly one original per quadruple, and RERELEASE=1 needs DEV_VERSION>=1 and that original, never carries GM=1 and is refused on main, and finalize_version_locked.sh writes it as RERELEASE=0 in version/locked, which check_version_locked_subset_of_entries.sh compares against (scripts/lib/ver_locked_form.sh); tests/test_version_scripts.sh covers each rule, and CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, docs/versioning.md, replit.md, the Cursor rule and .coderabbit.yaml state it.

RELEASE_DATE=2026-11-17
